it can be tough for a movie studio to  know when they've got a flop on their  hands until it's too late but once in a  while films come along that have  disaster written all over them in big  flashing neon letters long before  they're released the writing was on the  wall for these pictures well before they  made their way to theaters they're some  of the biggest box-office bombs of all  time and many of us saw them coming from  a mile away  Aeon Flux the live-action adaptation of  MTV's animated series Aeon Flux with  Charlize Theron in the title role  arrived a full decade after the last  episode of the show aired already facing  that uphill battle the film was shuffled  around through multiple regime changes  at Paramount studio head Sherry Lansing  initially told director Karyn Kusama  that she wanted it to be paramount  answer to Blade Runner but incoming  chief Gail Berman wanted more of a Buffy  the Vampire Slayer vibe after struggling  through the shoot with unclear  directives Kusama then endured the  ultimate insult as a third incoming  administration removed her from the  post-production process and in her words  eviscerated the film no when I reflect  back on it I'm like what was a thinking  about 25 minutes was sliced from  kazama's cut resulting in a barely  coherent movie who sama would later say  what they didn't realize is that  sometimes a cohesive 100 minutes will  feel much shorter than an incoherent and  barely comprehensible 82 it predictably  garnered appalling reviews and barely  registered at the box office  Cowboys and Aliens Cowboys and Aliens  seemed like the kind of high concept  romp that the right filmmaker could  knock out of the park and director Jon  Favreau fresh off the success of a Iron  Man and its sequel certainly seemed like  the man for the job with Steven  Spielberg as a producer in an all-star  cast including Daniel Craig and Harrison  Ford big things were in store for the  picture but they missed a couple of red  flags the script was labored over by  more than a dozen writers for 14 years  which is never a good sign it also  tracked poorly with its target audience  for the duration of its long marketing  campaign the biggest insult of all it  just barely managed to edge out the  Smurfs in its opening week I'm so sorry  I didn't realize a sad for you it  ultimately failed to make back its  production budget domestic  Cowboys and Aliens certainly had a  wealth of talent but everyone involved  forgot the crucial step of making sure  they were crafting a picture people  actually wanted to see Fantastic Four  production problems are a pretty  reliable indicator that a big-budget  tentpole film could be in trouble and  few have had production issues as  extensive as 2015's Fantastic Four from  questionable casting to clashes between  director Josh Trank and writer producer  Simon Kinberg rumors flew fast and  furious about the snafus plaguing  Fantastic Four four months before the  film's release tranq and Kinberg  publicly shot down these rumors but then  the film hit theaters and it became  astoundingly clear that every single  rumor had been true and then some  audiences stayed away and those who gave  it a chance mostly wished they had in  the wake of the hostile reviews Trank  defended his work via twitter by  implying that the studio had savaged the  film in editing  as for Kinberg he ignored the film's  troubled production history by placing  the blame for its failure on its tone  proving that some people fail to learn  lessons even when they're taught the  hard way JOHN CARTER Disney's John  Carter had a lot of things going for it  it was the start of an adaptation of a  series of classic influential sci-fi  novels by Edgar Rice Burroughs and  director Andrew Stanton had previous  hits with Pixar including wall-e and  Finding Nemo and it had the massive  production budget one would expect for a  tentpole film any or all of these things  would have been brilliant to play up in  the film's marketing so of course the  first trailer capitalized by mentioning  exactly none of them  the teaser appeared in July 2011 and it  wasn't until the 2012 Super Bowl that a  second trailer was released  unfortunately it wasn't much better  speaking with vulture an anonymous  former studio executive called John  Carter's marketing campaign the worst in  history saying it's almost as if they  went out of their way to not make us  care amazingly the problem was  compounded by Stan's complete loyalty to  the source material assuming that the  public at large would adore John Carter  as much as he did  irony but you are beautiful and you  fight like a fire this colossal  miscalculation plus the vague marketing  campaign equaled very few butts in the  seats and Carter became far and away the  biggest flop of 2012  The Lone Ranger when Disney's  live-action The Lone Ranger was  announced in 2010 it wasn't clear  whether audiences were up for a  retelling of The Adventures of the  Western hero plus a few feathers were  ruffled over the choices of army hammer  and the title role and the decidedly non  made of Johnny Depp as his Native  American sidekick Tonto the production  was briefly shut down over budget  concerns in 2011 but managed to get back  on track then in early 2012 fans were  treated to the first glimpse of the film  featuring Hammer and the famous mask and  Depp in the most egregiously terrible  Tonto makeup anyone could have imagined  the images quickly drew jeers giving  observers plenty of negative things to  talk about before the film was finally  released in July 2013 it failed to  generate any significant positive social  media buzz and analysts correctly  predicted that the film would be  trounced by Despicable Me 2 which opened  the same weekend and went on to gross  nearly a billion dollars  [Music]
 

